 0 is ahead by about +40. Stock also arrives earlier, which slightly cuts shortages.\n\n**Order levels against expected demand (days 2–29 for product 0, days 3–29 for product 1):**\n\n| Candidate | Product 0 ordered | Product 0 expected demand | Product 1 ordered | Product 1 expected demand |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 799 | ≈758 | 919 | ≈916 |\n| 2 | 870 | ≈758 | 876 | ≈916 |\n\n- Candidate 0 carries about 40 units of product 0 as a safety buffer and orders product 1 close to expected demand.\n- Candidate 2 over-orders product 0 by about 112 units. That costs about 570 in purchases plus holding on a growing stock, more than the shortages it prevents are worth. It also orders too little product 1.\n\n**The other candidates:**\n- **3 and 7** order product 0 at or below average demand, so they have more shortages, and they pay daily order fees.\n- **5** over-orders product 0 and also pays daily order fees.\n- **1** orders the same amount every day, so it doesn't follow peaks like the 48 on day 12, and it pays daily fees.\n- **6** orders every 3 days. That saves about 666 in fees but adds about 900 in holding, and total stock comes close to the 232 capacity, so units could be lost on arrival.\n\n**Robustness when demand differs from forecast.** Candidate 0 keeps a moderate buffer for product 0. Ordering for two days at once also means a high-demand first day draws on the stock meant for the second day, so it is less sensitive to random swings."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 354, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5699, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 354}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-19T07:53:54.237Z", "request_id": 